  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 AM EST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nA well defined upper-level trough is lifting north-northeast through \nKansas early this morning. Closer to home, low stratus and patchy \ndrizzle continues to lift north through Michigan in response to this \nlifting cyclone. Stout warm air advection has brought steadily \nrising temperatures on this shift with an increasing number of 60-\ndegree readings (normal high today is only 55). That said, morning \ntemperatures will be a nice spring board for afternoon highs that \nwill challenge the day's records: 76 degrees in 2003 and 1987 (2003 \nrecord was only at Fort Wayne). Rather cloudy immediately upstream, \nbut clearing is noted across the Ohio River. This could be enough \nlater today to truly challenge the record. Cloud cover will also \ndictate afternoon mixing of the low-level jet. Forecast soundings \ndepict a narrow window after 1pm where guidance attempts to mix down \n35 mph gusts. Overall today, breezy, decreasing clouds (especially \nalong and east of the I-69 corridor) and dry. \n\nRain slowly arrives from west to east Tuesday night according to \navailable near-term models (RIP HREF, NAM et. al.). Northwest Ohio \ncould very well be dry until after daybreak Wednesday as low \npressure becomes stacked beneath the parent upper-low, slowing the \ncold front's arrival. Breezy conditions persist into Wednesday \nmorning as the low-level jet remains amplified over northwest Ohio. \nA dry slot from this low slowly works its way east permitting some \nafternoon dry hours. Cooler air behind this system seeps in \nWednesday night through Thursday along with lake-enhanced showers. \nHighs in the 60s Wednesday and Thursday give way to highs in the 50s \nFriday through the weekend. This whole system exits later Saturday \nending the lake-enhanced shower chance.\n\nBeyond the weekend, subtle northwest flow results in temperatures \nfairly close to normal and opportunities for 20% POPs at times as \nweak disturbances ripple through the flow. Subtle ridging is noted \nover the southern and central US, which, if building into the \nMidwest could later induce a more zonal flow locally. However, the \ncore global models are teasing chilly Canadian High pressure after \nVeteran's Day.",
